      <h2 class="text-[28px] lg:text-[36px] font-bold text-[#1A1A1A] font-serif mb-6">
        Business Tax Liens in Utah
      </h2>
      <p class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65] mb-6">
        If your Utah business owes taxes, the IRS may file a Federal Tax Lien against you. Call our Business Tax Attorneys today — business tax liens can severely impact your company's ability to operate, obtain financing, and complete commercial transactions.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-[22px] lg:text-[24px] font-bold text-[#1A1A1A] font-serif mt-10 mb-4">
        How Business Tax Liens Differ from Individual Liens
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65] mb-4">
        Business tax liens present unique challenges:
      </p>
      <ul class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65] mb-6 space-y-2 list-disc pl-6">
        <li>They attach to all business assets, including accounts receivable, equipment, inventory, and real property</li>
        <li>They can affect the business owner's personal assets as well, particularly if the business is a sole proprietorship or if personal guarantees exist</li>
        <li>They appear in public records, which can damage supplier relationships and customer confidence</li>
        <li>Commercial lenders typically will not advance funds to businesses with tax liens</li>
        <li>Factoring companies and other financial partners often terminate relationships when liens appear</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-[22px] lg:text-[24px] font-bold text-[#1A1A1A] font-serif mt-10 mb-4">
        Resolution Options for Business Tax Liens
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65] mb-4">
        Our attorneys work with business owners to explore all available resolution strategies:
      </p>
      <ul class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65] mb-6 space-y-2 list-disc pl-6">
        <li><strong class="text-[#1A1A1A]">Full payment</strong> to obtain immediate lien release</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#1A1A1A]">Installment agreements</strong> with lien withdrawal provisions for qualifying businesses</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#1A1A1A]">Offer in Compromise</strong> to settle the debt for less than owed</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#1A1A1A]">Lien subordination</strong> to allow refinancing or new financing despite the lien</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#1A1A1A]">Certificate of discharge</strong> to remove the lien from specific assets so they can be sold</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-[22px] lg:text-[24px] font-bold text-[#1A1A1A] font-serif mt-10 mb-4">
        Protecting Your Business
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65] mb-6">
        The sooner you address a business tax lien, the more options you have. Waiting only allows interest and penalties to accumulate while your business's financial position worsens.
      </p>
